## Prerequisites<a id="prerequisites"></a>

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), or [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/) with
  [antsibull-nox](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/antsibull-nox/) in one
  Python environment.
- [Podman](https://podman.io/) for the container-backed `ansible-test` sessions.
- An authorized lab for each
  [Molecule scenario](./extensions/molecule/README.md).

## Checks<a id="checks"></a>

Run the checks from the collection's Git root. With nox installed, `nox`
replaces `uv run noxfile.py`.

```bash
uv run noxfile.py                 # fix, then run default checks
CI=true uv run noxfile.py         # check only
uv run noxfile.py -l              # list sessions
uv run noxfile.py -s lint         # Python, plugin YAML and typing
uv run noxfile.py -s helper-lint  # Python helpers
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-units
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-integration
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule
```

Among the default sessions:

- `runtime-check` installs the built collection in isolation and keeps only
  what ships: `meta/`, `plugins/`, `roles/` and `playbooks/`. It checks Python
  imports, module documentation and the syntax of any roles, then runs the
  unit tests against that installation. To check the exact archive of a
  release:

  ```sh
  uv run noxfile.py -s runtime-check -- --artifact /path/to/collection.tar.gz
  ```

- `helper-lint` checks the Python outside `plugins/` with
  [ruff.toml](ruff.toml): `extensions/molecule/`, any role's `files/`,
  `tools/`, `scripts/` and `tests/runtime_check.py`. Add it to CI
  configurations that list sessions explicitly.

Session definitions: [noxfile.py](noxfile.py).
Built-in session settings: [antsibull-nox.toml](antsibull-nox.toml).
While editing, run the sessions of one ansible-core release, such as
`uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-units-2.21` (`-l` lists them), and the
default sessions before a commit.

## Unit-test clocks<a id="unit-test-clocks"></a>

Use the opt-in `fake_clock` fixture when a test's expected outcome depends
on polling or a short deadline. It keeps the real `foundata.utils`
`Deadline`, but replaces its module-local clock. Patch each polling module
before invoking the code under test:

```python
fake_clock.patch(_activation)
```

`fake_clock.sleep(seconds)` advances virtual time immediately.
`fake_clock.advance(seconds)` models work in a fake handler, and
`fake_clock.now` exposes elapsed time. Budget the deadline for these
deliberate advances, not for host speed. Tests that do not request the
fixture keep real time; pytest restores all patches after each test.

`patch(module)` replaces that module's `time` reference, not Python's
global time functions. Modules that import a clock function directly or
capture it in a default argument need explicit injection instead.
Use this fixture only with synchronous fake products, not real I/O,
background workers or event loops. See `test_activation.py` for complete
polling and timeout examples.

## Boundaries<a id="boundaries"></a>

A resource is one kind of product object and the module that manages it.
A definition in `_definitions.py` describes a definition-driven resource,
and `_engine.py` runs it. A hook-based resource needs more than a definition
can express, such as nested values, so its own module utility implements the
lifecycle's hooks. A subsystem is a part of the product that a group of
modules manages together, such as routes and gateways with their activation.
Its modules share one module utility, one activation scope where the product
has one, and usually one Molecule scenario.

`tests/unit/plugins/modules/contract_subject.py` names the product resource,
fake adapters and entry points the shared contract tests run against.
A new hook-based resource starts as a
copy of the skeleton's `_fixture_nested.py` with its module and tests and
changes only the hook methods; the order of the lifecycle steps belongs to the
base. The shared base and engine tests bring their own synthetic resource and
fake product, so adapting the contract subject needs no change to them.

Some files come from foundata's module skeleton and are the same in every
collection rendered from it:

- `plugins/module_utils/`: `_lifecycle.py`, `_engine.py` and `_activation.py`.
- `tests/unit/plugins/`: `conftest.py` (including `fake_clock`), and
  `test_contract.py`, `test_engine.py`, `test_activation.py` and
  `test_lifecycle.py` in `modules/`.
- `extensions/molecule/shared/`: everything.
- `tests/integration/targets/setup_loopback/`: everything.

Leave them unchanged, so that you can update them from a newer render of
[foundata/ansible-skeletons](https://github.com/foundata/ansible-skeletons);
a local edit would conflict with that update. When one of them needs a
change, make it in the skeleton so that every collection receives it.
[CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/foundata/ansible-skeletons/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)
explains how to propose one.

{# skeleton-variant: appliance-invariants #}
{# end skeleton-variant #}
Two files connect the shared code to this product and keep the product's
values when the skeleton changes: `contract_subject.py` points the shared
contract tests at a product resource, and `plugins/module_utils/_binding.py`
gives the lifecycle base this collection's client, error schema and
compatibility check.

`foundata.utils` owns byte transport, deadlines, JSON decoding and safe error
translation. Everything else is product-owned, including the client, the
definitions, the fake product and the scenario hooks.

File-only API fixtures use the hidden `setup_loopback` integration target.
Add it to the product setup target's dependencies, then load
`setup_loopback/files/server.py` beside that target and call `serve`.
The product supplies its routes and shutdown authentication; the helper owns
HTTP dispatch, the loopback listener, the lifetime timer and the port file.
Its docstring describes the callbacks and configuration.

The inventory extractor writes `tools/inventory/<series>.yml` beside itself.
That inventory is development evidence: it never ships and nothing reads it
at runtime.

